<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<argument>
  <properties>
    <title>
      <![CDATA[New Argument]]>
    </title>
    <author>
      <![CDATA[]]>
    </author>
    <version>
      <![CDATA[]]>
    </version>
    <date>
      <![CDATA[]]>
    </date>
    <status>
      <![CDATA[]]>
    </status>
    <prefix>
      <![CDATA[]]>
    </prefix>
    <confidence>
      <![CDATA[false]]>
    </confidence>
    <note>
      <![CDATA[]]>
    </note>
  </properties>

  <gsn>
    <g
        id="__gsn__19611d2a36e"
        color="#ffffff"
        name="Zone Alert Will Work">

      <s
          id="__gsn__19611d30420"
          color="#ffffff"
          name="Strategy">
        Simple "back-of-the-envelope" algorithm argument.

        <g
            id="__gsn__19611d3087e"
            color="#ffffff"
            name="We Can Get The Live Info We Need">
          From messages sent out in OpenUxAS simulations.

          <s
              id="__gsn__19611dd2863"
              color="#ffffff"
              name="Strategy">
            By the message types we need

            <g
                id="__gsn__19611dd2cd2"
                color="#ffffff"
                name="Vehicle Info">
              We can get vehicle state showing position, direction, velocity, etc.

              <e
                  id="__gsn__19611de396b"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="OpenUXAS Messages">
                .
              </e>
            </g>

            <g
                id="__gsn__19611dd2f07"
                color="#ffffff"
                name="Zone Info">
              We can get zone declarations that each declare the geometry of a keep-in and keep-out zone.

              <e
                  id="__gsn__19611de3c7c"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="OpenUXAS Messages">
                .
              </e>
            </g>
          </s>
        </g>

        <g
            id="__gsn__19611d30b05"
            color="#ffffff"
            name="We Can Compute the Zone Boundaaries">
          We can convert the declared zones into merged zones on the plane by copying or re-using the code of the RoutePlannerVisibilityService

          <e
              id="__gsn__19611df2bac"
              color="#ffffff"
              name="RoutePlannerVisibilityService.h and cpp">
            .
          </e>
        </g>

        <g
            id="__gsn__19611dfb799"
            color="#ffffff"
            name="We Can Compute Alerts">
          We can compute zone alerts as desired.

          <g
              id="__gsn__19611e00bf4"
              color="#ffffff"
              name="Trigger on Vehicle State Updates">
            We can trigger zone alerts whenever a vehicle reports a state message.

            <e
                id="__gsn__19611e11877"
                color="#ffffff"
                name="Best Info">
              This is the most accurate update on where a vehicle is.
            </e>
          </g>

          <g
              id="__gsn__19611e00da1"
              color="#ffffff"
              name="Zone Conflict Computation">
            From the vehicle state message, we can compute conflicts with zones on the plane./

            <g
                id="__gsn__19611e1b5ef"
                color="#ffffff"
                name="Compute Active Conflict">
              By  checking if vehicle ground-plane position is inside the horizontal boundary of each zone.

              <c
                  id="__gsn__19611e421fe"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="Active Conflict">
                A conflict where the reported vehicle position is in conflict with a zone.
              </c>

              <g
                  id="__gsn__19611e2c274"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="Easy to Know if Inside a Zone">
                By  checking if vehicle ground-plane position is inside the horizontal boundary of each zone.

                <e
                    id="__gsn__19611e30509"
                    color="#ffffff"
                    name="Napkin">
                  See simple drawing
                </e>
              </g>

              <g
                  id="__gsn__19611e2c470"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="Keep Out Zone Active Conflict">
                We know if a vehicle is in active conflict with a keep-out zone if the vehicle is inside or on the keep-out zone ground-plane polygon.

                <e
                    id="__gsn__19611e3d1e7"
                    color="#ffffff"
                    name="Napkin">
                  See simple drawing
                </e>
              </g>

              <g
                  id="__gsn__19611e3d92c"
                  color="#ffcccc"
                  name="Keep-In Zone Active Conflict">
                We can detect a keep-in zone conflict as one where a vehicle was inside or on a keep-out zone polygon in the horizontal plane, and is no outside of that polygon.

                <e
                    id="__gsn__19611e512a5"
                    color="#ffffff"
                    name="Napkin">
                  See simple drawing
                </e>
              </g>
            </g>

            <g
                id="__gsn__19611e54884"
                color="#ffffff"
                name="Compute Imminent Conflict">
              By computing whether the vehicle's linear trajectory within a look-ahead time conflicts with a zone.

              <g
                  id="__gsn__19611e1b2e1"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="Compute Linear Trajectory">
                From vehicle's velocity against the ground plane

                <e
                    id="__gsn__19611e208fe"
                    color="#ffffff"
                    name="Napkin">
                  See simple drawing
                </e>
              </g>

              <g
                  id="__gsn__19611e5e9df"
                  color="#ffffff"
                  name="Keep-Out Zone Imminent Conflict">
                We can compute if, when, and where a vehicle is in imminent keep-out zone conflict if its trajectory from its reported position to future position at lookahead time crosses onto or inside a keep-out zone polygon.

                <e
                    id="__gsn__19611e6d9cb"
                    color="#ffffff"
                    name="Napkin">
                  See simple drawing
                </e>
              </g>

              <g
                  id="__gsn__19611e6e2e3"
                  color="#ffcccc"
                  name="Keep-In Zone Imminent Conflict">
                We can compute if, when, and where a vehicle is in imminent keep-in zone conflict if its reported position is inside a keep-in zone and the trajectory from its reported position to future position at lookahead time crosses outside of the keep-in zone.

                <e
                    id="__gsn__19611e6e2e4"
                    color="#ffffff"
                    name="Napkin">
                  See simple drawing
                </e>
              </g>
            </g>
          </g>
        </g>
      </s>
    </g>
  </gsn>
</argument>
